Thanks Laura.  That is a pretty difficult problem -- there's a big problem of credit assignment between the random intercept and the manipulation, due to the between-subjects design and the huge cross-subject variation.  This is the kind of dataset where I'm not sure how far I would trust the results even if lmer told me that the model converged.

Something as simple as Fisher's exact test, dichotomizing between participants with >=0.5 response and <0.5 response, probably gets you most of the way to an answer to your question of whether your Dative and Transitive conditions differ:

  fisher.test(matrix(c(12,4,8,8),2,2))

which gives a p-value of 0.2734. Counting subject 19 as a 0 rather than a 1 gives a p-value of 0.1351.

I'd also be interested to know more about the GEE approach Dale mentions (I've heard about these techniques but don't know much about them), and find out the results that they give.

Best & hope this helps.

Roger



